# Troubleshooting {{ project_name }}

The problems people actually hit, each with its fix. Commands are for this project, so they can be copied as they are. Windows commands are for PowerShell.

## The everyday commands

```bash
uv sync                                              # install or refresh this project
uv run --extra dev pytest                            # run the tests
uv run {{ project_slug }} install claude-desktop             # add to a client (or cursor, claude-code, codex, all)
uv run {{ project_slug }} install claude-desktop --writes    # the same, with write tools on
uv run {{ project_slug }} install claude-desktop --remove    # take it out again
uv run {{ project_slug }} install all --dry-run              # show what would change, change nothing
uv run {{ project_slug }}                                    # run the server by hand (it waits silently for a client; Ctrl+C to stop)
```

Once it's deployed (`docs/remote.md`):

```bash
uv run {{ project_slug }} deploy vercel --auth supabase --create-supabase-project --writes   # first deploy
uv run {{ project_slug }} deploy vercel --auth supabase --writes                             # every later deploy
uv run {{ project_slug }} users add ada@example.com bob@example.com --writer                 # let people sign in (spaces or commas)
uv run {{ project_slug }} users grant bob@example.com                                        # make a writer
uv run {{ project_slug }} users revoke bob@example.com                                       # back to reader
uv run {{ project_slug }} users list
```

## Connecting a client

| You see | Why | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| The server doesn't show up in Claude Desktop | Claude Desktop only reads its config at startup, and closing the window doesn't quit it | Quit it fully: Windows, right-click the tray icon (bottom right, maybe behind the ^ arrow) and choose Quit; macOS, Claude > Quit Claude. Then reopen it |
| Still not there after a full quit | Newer Windows installs keep the config outside `%APPDATA%` | `install claude-desktop` updates every config it finds; run it again and check the paths it prints. They're also listed under "Where the configs live" below |
| Codex or another client says the server "failed to start" or timed out | The first launch was still installing the project | Run `uv sync` once in this folder, then start the client again |
| The client calls an older server with the same tools | An earlier test server is still registered, often under another name | Name this server in the request ("using {{ project_slug }}"), or remove the old entry from the client |
| `uv run {{ project_slug }}` prints "Registered tools" and then nothing | That's normal: over stdio the server waits for a client to talk to it | Press Ctrl+C. Clients start it for you; you don't need to keep it running |

## Writes and confirmations

| You see | Why | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| The write tools (create, update, delete) aren't there | Writes are off by default, so those tools aren't registered | `uv run {{ project_slug }} install <client> --writes`, then restart the client |
| "Writes are disabled" in the server's log when you run it by hand | `{{ env_prefix }}_ENABLE_WRITES` isn't set in your terminal | Expected. The client passes it from its config when it starts the server |
| An update runs without asking, but delete asks | The default policy (`confirm: destructive`) only asks before destructive tools | Set `confirm: all_writes` in your policy to be asked before every write |
| "...was not run: it needs the user's confirmation, and this connection can't carry a confirmation prompt" | The client can't show the prompt, so Sett refuses instead of guessing | Use a client that shows prompts (see [Sett's client support table](https://github.com/kclarkedesign/sett-mcp#client-support)). In Claude Desktop, local servers can't show them but remote ones can: deploy it (`docs/remote.md`) |
| "...the confirmation was cancelled" | The prompt was dismissed, or the client couldn't show it | Try again and answer the prompt |
| "...the user declined" | Someone chose Deny or Decline | Nothing to fix: the tool didn't run |
| "...an argument is, or contains, the placeholder '[masked]'" | `[masked]` stands in for a hidden value, so writing it back would overwrite the real one. In a free-text field you scan with `mask_patterns`, any text that contains `[masked]` counts, because the model read "call me at [masked]" and is sending that text back | Ask for the change with the real text, and have the model pass only the fields it means to change. To edit a note with hidden parts, write the whole note again without the placeholder |
| The server stops at startup with "Invalid policy" naming `mask_patterns` | A key or kind in that section is misspelled (`feilds`, `kinds: [emial]`), or `fields` or `kinds` is empty. The policy refuses to start rather than quietly hide nothing | Use exactly `fields` and `kinds`; the kinds are `email` and `phone`, and neither list may be empty (leave `kinds` out to use both) |

## Deploying (`deploy vercel --auth supabase`)

| You see | Why | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| PowerShell: "vercel.ps1 cannot be loaded because running scripts is disabled" | Windows blocks the launcher npm installed | `Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned` once, or type `vercel.cmd`. The deploy itself isn't affected |
| The deploy waits on a step with no output | The Vercel CLI asked a question (older CLIs) | Answer it in the terminal; the deploy carries on |
| "The server ... didn't come up (HTTP 404)" | Vercel didn't run the app as Python | Check `vercel.json` (`"framework": "python"`) and `asgi.py` are in this folder |
| "didn't come up" with another error | A setting is missing or wrong | `vercel logs --deployment <your-project>.vercel.app` names it |
| "Which Supabase organization?" | You belong to more than one | Add `--supabase-org` with the name in quotes (`--supabase-org "My Org"`) or the slug from the list. Choose a free organization unless you mean to pay: a project in a paid one adds to its bill |
| Creating the project is refused, mentioning the free plan | The free plan allows two active projects across the organizations you own or administer | Pause or delete one in the Supabase dashboard, or use `--supabase-project <ref>` with an existing project |
| "isn't ready after 15 minutes" | The new project is still starting | Run the same deploy again later: the project was saved, so it carries on instead of creating another |
| It asks for a Supabase access token although you ran `supabase login` | The deploy couldn't read the CLI's saved sign-in (a different profile, or a keyring it can't open) | Set `SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN`, or paste a token from Account, Access Tokens |
| "still signs tokens with a shared secret" | The Supabase project uses the legacy JWT secret | Supabase dashboard, Authentication, JWT Keys, "Rotate keys"; then deploy again |
| "This Supabase project's Site URL is ..." | The project already serves another site | Use a Supabase project just for this server, or `--yes` if that address was this server's |
| No rows in the `sett_audit` table, "403" in Vercel's logs | Supabase's Data API is off, or the table predates Sett 0.3.0 | Turn on the Data API (Project Settings, Data API), and run `grant insert on table public.sett_audit to service_role;` in the SQL editor |
| New audit rows stopped arriving after you updated the project, and Vercel's logs say "Could not write audit row ... HTTP Error 400" | The table is older than the code: it lacks the `client_name`, `client_version` and `user_agent` columns the server now writes, and Supabase refuses a row with a column it doesn't have | Run the deploy again (`deploy vercel --auth supabase`): it adds them before the new code goes live. Or paste this in Supabase's SQL Editor and press Run: `alter table public.sett_audit add column if not exists client_name text, add column if not exists client_version text, add column if not exists user_agent text;` Rows written in between aren't kept |
| Signing in says "Signups not allowed" | The email wasn't added, or GitHub's email differs from the one added | `uv run {{ project_slug }} users add <email>` with the address the person signs in with (several at once: separate them with spaces or commas) |
| The connector says it can't connect, or the consent page never opens | The address is missing `/mcp`, or a popup blocker stopped the sign-in window | Use the full `https://<your-project>.vercel.app/mcp` and allow popups for claude.ai |
| The sign-in email never arrives | Supabase's built-in sender only mails members of your Supabase organization, a few times an hour | Add your own SMTP server (Supabase, Authentication, Emails), or use GitHub sign-in |
| A user can read but not write | They don't have the writer role, or their sign-in predates it | `uv run {{ project_slug }} users grant <email>`; it applies within an hour, or at once if they sign in again |
| Everything stopped working after a week | Free Supabase projects pause when idle | Restore the project in the Supabase dashboard |

## Testing a deployed server

```bash
uvx sett-mcp check https://<your-project>.vercel.app/mcp
```

It prints one line per check, starting with PASS, WARN, FAIL or INFO, and a fix under anything that needs one. A FAIL means current clients would hit that problem too, and the command ends with an error code, so it also works in a script. Add `--token -` to check the tools as a signed-in user (`docs/remote.md` says what it needs). Nothing on the server changes.

| You see | Why | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `FAIL Requires sign-in` with "got HTTP 403, not 401" | Something answers requests that have no token with 403. Clients only start signing in after a 401 with a `WWW-Authenticate` header, so with a 403 they never offer the sign-in | Answer requests without a token with 401 and a `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata="..."` header. 403 is for someone who is signed in but not allowed. A firewall or access gateway in front of the server is the usual cause: let `/mcp` through to the server, which sends the 401 itself |
| `WARN 401 points to sign-in` with "no Bearer challenge" or "No resource_metadata in the Bearer challenge" | The 401 doesn't carry a `Bearer` challenge with a `resource_metadata` link. Only the Bearer challenge counts, as in clients: a `Basic` or other scheme with the same word on it doesn't | Send `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata="https://<your-project>.vercel.app/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp"`. Clients can still find the metadata at that well-known address, but the link saves them guessing. Sett's server sends it, so a proxy in front that rewrites headers may be dropping it |
| `FAIL Protocol 2026-07-28` with "server/discover answered HeaderMismatch", "MissingRequiredClientCapability" or "UnsupportedProtocolVersion" | The server understood the current protocol and refused the request. Clients take that to mean it speaks 2026-07-28 and don't fall back to the older handshake, so they fail even when the handshake line says PASS | Update the MCP SDK (`uv lock --upgrade-package mcp`, then `uv sync`, then deploy again). With a proxy in front, make sure it passes the `Mcp-Method` and `Mcp-Protocol-Version` headers through unchanged |
| `FAIL Handshake protocol` with "notifications/initialized answered HTTP 404" (or 401, 500) | After `initialize`, older clients send one more message to say setup is done, and stop if it fails. A 404 usually means the server runs as several copies and the second one didn't know the session | Run the server stateless (Sett's `serve --http` does), or fix whatever answers with that error. A 401 means that step lacks the sign-in |
| `FAIL Tool definitions` mentioning `x-mcp-header` | A tool asks for a parameter to be copied into an HTTP header (`x-mcp-header`) but breaks a rule, and current clients leave the tool out | Use a plain header name (letters, digits and `-`) on a string, integer or boolean parameter that sits directly in the tool's `properties` (not inside `anyOf` or `items`), and don't use the same name twice (`Region` and `region` count as the same) |

## Where the configs live

| Client | Config | Written by |
|---|---|---|
| Claude Desktop, Windows | `%APPDATA%\Claude\claude_desktop_config.json`, or for newer installs `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Claude-Data\claude_*\claude_desktop_config.json` | `install claude-desktop` |
| Claude Desktop, macOS | `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` | `install claude-desktop` |
| Cursor | `~/.cursor/mcp.json` (Windows: `%USERPROFILE%\.cursor\mcp.json`) | `install cursor` |
| Claude Code | Managed by Claude Code; `claude mcp list` shows it | `install claude-code` (runs `claude mcp add`) |
| Codex | `~/.codex/config.toml` (Windows: `%USERPROFILE%\.codex\config.toml`) | `install codex` |

Each install backs up the file it changes as `<name>.bak` beside it, so a change can be undone by copying the backup back.

## Still stuck

Ask in [Sett's Discussions](https://github.com/kclarkedesign/sett-mcp/discussions/categories/q-a). Include your Sett version (`uvx sett-mcp --version`), OS, client and the command you ran with its output. Never paste access tokens, keys or `.env` contents.
